facts:
On a grid, two vectors are equal when the same step across and up takes you from each tail to its head.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[help lines] (0,0) grid (7,5);
\draw[->,thick] (0,0) -- (3,2) node[midway,above left] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\draw[->,thick] (4,3) -- (7,5) node[midway,above left] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\draw[->,thick] (7,2) -- (4,0) node[midway,below right] {$-\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
Which two of the vectors shown are equal?
Give a reason.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[help lines] (0,0) grid (8,6);
\draw[->,thick] (0,0) -- (2,3) node[midway,left] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\draw[->,thick] (3,0) -- (5,2) node[midway,below right] {$\underset{\sim}{b}$};
\draw[->,thick] (6,0) -- (8,3) node[midway,right] {$\underset{\sim}{c}$};
\draw[->,thick] (2,6) -- (0,3) node[midway,left] {$\underset{\sim}{d}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Sketch $-\underset{\sim}{p}$ on the grid.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[help lines] (0,0) grid (6,5);
\draw[->,thick] (1,1) -- (3,4) node[midway,left] {$\underset{\sim}{p}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
$\underset{\sim}{p}$ steps $2$ right and $3$ up.
Its negative has the same length and the opposite direction, so draw an arrow $2$ left and $3$ down.
One possible sketch is shown.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[help lines] (0,0) grid (6,5);
\draw[->,thick] (5,4) -- (3,1) node[midway,right] {$-\underset{\sim}{p}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

